// WHERE A TARGET'S SURFACES LIVE IS ORGANIZATIONAL POLICY, NEVER IDENTITY (operator verdict relayed
// 2026-08-05). DeclarationRef remains the single identity authority for a language target; a
// canonical_prefix says where that target's modules SHOULD be stored and decides nothing about what
// the target IS. A path prefix is exactly the positional fact that would compete with the symbolic
// key if it became a discriminant — the class DESIGN section 3 rules on: paths are discriminators,
// not gospel. WHAT THIS MODULE ENFORCES, AS NARROWLY AS IT IS TRUE (operator REQUEST_CHANGES,
// 2026-08-05): a file ADDED under dag/extdeps/languages/ must sit inside a DECLARED TARGET
// DIRECTORY, or be one of the four exact legacy flat modules — a directory-membership ratchet. IT
// IS NOT A TARGET-PAIRING WALL; an earlier revision's law read 'a module misfiled under another
// target's directory is therefore REFUSED', which was false of the shipped mechanism. The gate
// observes added PATH STRINGS and nothing supplies or resolves a module's declared
// extdeps_model_scope.subject, so dag/extdeps/languages/html/verilog_emit.dag is ADMITTED — html/
// is a declared home — even if it declares the Verilog subject. WHY THE OVERCLAIM SURVIVED REVIEW:
// a witness did exercise the relational property, but associated the path with the HTML home by
// reading html/ OUT OF THE PATH — the path-to-identity fusion this note forbids, performed by the
// control meant to prove it never happens. A correct control names verilog_language_subject_ref
// and demands the Verilog prefix; it cannot be written against a caller holding only a string. THE
// RELATIONAL WALL IS A SEPARATE CHANGE needing a real join: a placement observation carrying
// (file_path, target) resolved from each added carrier's own scope, looked up in this roster by
// THAT target. The predicates that decided the relational case correctly were DELETED rather than
// left beside an unwired claim: machinery kept for a future consumer is the inert-lens tier DESIGN
// section 6 names, and this module already shipped one.

// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES AND DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. IT DOES: declare a canonical home per
// registered language target, carry the four legacy flat modules as an EXACT finite frontier with a
// dissolution trigger each, and supply the admission predicate tools.extdeps_scope_placement_gate
// calls on the paths a change adds. IT DOES NOT: move any file, edit any importer, touch src/v2, or
// check that a module sits under ITS OWN target's home rather than someone else's. IT ALSO NO
// LONGER CARRIES EXCEPTIONS FOR UNREGISTERED-TARGET DIRECTORIES (operator P1, 2026-08-05). An
// earlier revision declared dag/extdeps/languages/gpu/ and dag/extdeps/languages/simd/ as
// admitted-because-unregistered. The gate observes only NEWLY ADDED paths, so existing files under
// them never needed an exception — the rows bought nothing for the present tree and granted a
// permanent licence for unlimited FUTURE files under an uncited subtree: bounded in rows, unbounded
// in effect. New work under an unregistered language should force the registration, which their
// absence now does. NGSPICE IS ALSO OUT OF THIS WALL'S POPULATION: the roster once declared a
// simulator home under dag/extdeps/simulators/, while the predicate judges only
// dag/extdeps/languages/, so that row passed by never being consulted — an unenforced claim,
// removed. WHY THE FLAT-MODULE MOVES ARE NOT MADE HERE: all four are rows of the FROZEN legacy
// manifest in gunbc.extdeps_scope_frontier, and an added dag/extdeps path can only land as scope
// carrier or genuine machinery, never rejoin the manifest — so each move forces its surface module
// to spell a second literal ExternalAuthority for an upstream whose subject carrier D0 just
// authored. That question is escalated in extdeps.posix.shell_command_language
// posix_authority_duplication_note.

